I. INTRODUCTION
Phase transitions represent fundamental phenomena across diverse physical systems, from magnetization in spin systems to collective motion in biological swarms [1]. Traditional approaches to phase transition prediction rely on equilibrium statistical mechanics and mean-field theories, which often fail to capture the complex dynamics and fluctuations preceding critical transitions [2]. Recent advances in machine learning have opened new avenues for understanding complex systems [3]. However, most existing approaches treat phase transition prediction as a standard classification problem, overlooking two crucial aspects: (1) the temporal correlations that characterize approach to criticality, and (2) the role of rare fluctuations as harbingers of impending transitions [4]. In this work, we introduce a framework that synergistically combines attention mechanisms from deep learning with rare event detection from large deviation theory. Our approach recognizes that phase transitions are preceded by characteristic patterns in both typical behavior (captured by attention) and atypical fluctuations (identified through rare event analysis).
